1
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:12,731
We are following one number as we apply p again and again.

2
00:00:12,731 --> 00:00:17,024
p of x is three halves x, minus one half x cubed.

3
00:00:17,124 --> 00:00:19,226
Here is what we know so far.

4
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:24,626
A negative value moves exactly like its positive twin, with the sign flipped.

5
00:00:24,626 --> 00:00:27,418
We called that the mirror rule.

6
00:00:27,518 --> 00:00:33,936
Past square root of three, p of x is negative, so a step flips the sign.

7
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:39,390
And one step multiplies the size of the value by the size factor,

8
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:42,026
x squared minus three, over two.

9
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:49,372
So below square root of three, everything goes to one,

10
00:00:49,372 --> 00:00:53,342
and beyond square root of five, everything explodes.

11
00:00:53,342 --> 00:00:55,622
That leaves the gap between them.

12
00:00:55,622 --> 00:00:59,516
In the gap, every step flips the sign.

13
00:00:59,616 --> 00:01:01,388
What about the size?

14
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:07,476
One step multiplies it by the size factor, x squared minus three, over two.

15
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:12,656
At the left end of the gap, x squared is three, so the factor is zero.

16
00:01:12,656 --> 00:01:17,288
At the right end, x squared is five, so the factor is one.

17
00:01:17,288 --> 00:01:21,453
In between, x squared is between three and five,

18
00:01:21,453 --> 00:01:24,752
so the factor is between zero and one.

19
00:01:24,852 --> 00:01:29,926
Multiplying by a number between zero and one makes a size smaller.

20
00:01:29,926 --> 00:01:34,860
So in the gap, the size of p of x is less than the size of x.

21
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:39,124
Every step brings the value closer to zero.

22
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:42,422
That suggests a plan.

23
00:01:42,422 --> 00:01:47,420
Suppose the size shrinks far enough to drop below square root of three.

24
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,282
Inside square root of three we already know everything.

25
00:01:51,282 --> 00:01:53,980
A positive value goes to plus one.

26
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:58,804
By the mirror rule, a negative value goes to minus one.

27
00:01:58,904 --> 00:02:02,936
So we only have to follow a start until it gets inside,

28
00:02:02,936 --> 00:02:05,356
and see on which side it arrives.

29
00:02:06,783 --> 00:02:08,995
Try it on the cobweb picture.

30
00:02:08,995 --> 00:02:11,147
Start at two point zero.

31
00:02:11,147 --> 00:02:14,507
Go down to the curve, which gives minus one.

32
00:02:14,507 --> 00:02:16,801
Then across to the diagonal.

33
00:02:16,801 --> 00:02:22,339
Minus one is inside, on the negative side, and it is already a fixed point.

34
00:02:22,339 --> 00:02:27,285
So two point zero ends at minus one, after one flip.

35
00:02:27,716 --> 00:02:29,498
Now two point two.

36
00:02:29,498 --> 00:02:33,248
Down to the curve, at minus two point zero two.

37
00:02:33,248 --> 00:02:38,668
That is smaller in size than two point two, but it is still outside.

38
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:42,176
So go across to the diagonal and step again.

39
00:02:42,176 --> 00:02:46,206
This time the curve sends it up, to plus one point one one.

40
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:51,720
Now it is inside, on the positive side, and from there it settles at plus one.

41
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:55,002
Two flips, and a different ending.

42
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:58,846
And two point two three, just a little further out.

43
00:02:58,846 --> 00:03:01,266
Minus two point two zero.

44
00:03:01,266 --> 00:03:03,946
Then plus two point zero two.

45
00:03:03,946 --> 00:03:06,760
Then minus one point one zero.

46
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:12,059
It takes three flips to get inside, it arrives on the negative side,

47
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:14,008
and it ends at minus one.

48
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:18,382
So the starts in the gap do not all end the same way.

49
00:03:18,382 --> 00:03:21,828
What matters is how many flips it takes to get inside.

50
00:03:21,828 --> 00:03:26,316
Every start here is positive, and each flip changes the sign.

51
00:03:26,316 --> 00:03:31,668
After an odd number of flips the value arrives negative, and ends at minus one.

52
00:03:31,668 --> 00:03:36,796
After an even number it arrives positive, and ends at plus one.

53
00:03:38,016 --> 00:03:42,798
Three starts are not the whole story, so here is every start at once.

54
00:03:42,798 --> 00:03:47,281
Take each start from zero to two point four, run the iteration,

55
00:03:47,281 --> 00:03:49,416
and color it by where it ends.

56
00:03:49,416 --> 00:03:56,036
Teal means plus one, gold means minus one, and red means it explodes.

57
00:03:57,483 --> 00:04:01,683
Below square root of three it is all teal, as we showed.

58
00:04:01,683 --> 00:04:03,755
The gap begins gold.

59
00:04:03,755 --> 00:04:07,615
That is where two point zero sits, with its one flip.

60
00:04:07,615 --> 00:04:11,657
Then comes a thin teal stripe, which holds two point two.

61
00:04:11,657 --> 00:04:15,680
And then more stripes, each thinner than the last,

62
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:18,899
squeezed up against square root of five.

63
00:04:20,816 --> 00:04:23,366
So each stripe is a flip count.

64
00:04:23,366 --> 00:04:27,608
One flip, two flips, three flips, and so on.

65
00:04:27,608 --> 00:04:29,480
Two things are still open.

66
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:34,152
Where exactly does one flip turn into two, and two into three?

67
00:04:34,152 --> 00:04:37,603
And do these stripes really fill the whole gap,

68
00:04:37,603 --> 00:04:40,392
all the way up to square root of five?

69
00:04:43,116 --> 00:04:47,374
Following the sign and the size together on this picture gets messy.

70
00:04:47,374 --> 00:04:50,388
But the mirror rule lets us take them apart.

71
00:04:50,388 --> 00:04:56,538
A negative value moves exactly like its positive twin, with the sign flipped.

72
00:04:56,638 --> 00:04:59,440
So the size follows a rule of its own.

73
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,554
The next size is the size of p of x.

74
00:05:02,554 --> 00:05:05,016
And the sign we can simply count.

75
00:05:05,016 --> 00:05:10,236
Every step that starts outside square root of three is one flip.

76
00:05:10,683 --> 00:05:12,559
So fold the picture.

77
00:05:12,559 --> 00:05:16,263
We only need positive sizes, so look at the right half.

78
00:05:16,263 --> 00:05:20,379
Wherever the curve dips below the axis, flip that part up.

79
00:05:20,379 --> 00:05:23,599
This folded curve answers one question.

80
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:28,335
If the size is x now, what is the size after one step?

81
00:05:28,783 --> 00:05:32,971
Now compare it with the diagonal, where the size would stay the same.

82
00:05:32,971 --> 00:05:36,657
Between square root of three and square root of five,

83
00:05:36,657 --> 00:05:39,369
the folded curve is below the diagonal.

84
00:05:39,369 --> 00:05:43,247
The size after the step is smaller than the size before.

85
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:49,151
Beyond square root of five it is above the diagonal, and the size grows.

86
00:05:49,251 --> 00:05:53,191
They meet exactly at square root of five.

87
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:56,786
And the cobweb works on the folded picture too.

88
00:05:56,786 --> 00:05:58,738
Start at two point two.

89
00:05:58,738 --> 00:06:02,392
Up to the folded curve, at two point zero two.

90
00:06:02,392 --> 00:06:08,324
Across to the diagonal, and then to the curve again, at one point one one.

91
00:06:08,424 --> 00:06:12,174
Now we are inside, and the size climbs to one.

92
00:06:12,174 --> 00:06:16,192
Two of these steps started outside square root of three.

93
00:06:16,192 --> 00:06:20,514
So two flips, and the sign ends up positive.

94
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:25,180
Now zoom in on the gap, and stretch it sideways so we can see.

95
00:06:25,180 --> 00:06:27,508
Start with the simplest case.

96
00:06:27,508 --> 00:06:30,284
Which starts get inside in a single step?

97
00:06:30,284 --> 00:06:34,792
Those whose size after one step is below square root of three.

98
00:06:34,792 --> 00:06:38,670
So draw that level as a horizontal line.

99
00:06:38,770 --> 00:06:42,214
How often does the folded curve cross this line?

100
00:06:42,214 --> 00:06:45,910
Look at its formula, x times the size factor.

101
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:49,560
As x moves to the right through the gap, x gets bigger,

102
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:51,950
and the size factor gets bigger too.

103
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:54,246
So their product only goes up.

104
00:06:54,246 --> 00:06:57,528
It starts at height zero, at square root of three,

105
00:06:57,528 --> 00:07:00,286
and it ends at height square root of five.

106
00:07:00,286 --> 00:07:03,262
Our line lies between those two heights.

107
00:07:03,262 --> 00:07:07,072
And a curve that only goes up passes each height once.

108
00:07:07,072 --> 00:07:11,096
So it crosses the line at exactly one point.

109
00:07:11,196 --> 00:07:14,384
Call that start b one, b for boundary.

110
00:07:14,384 --> 00:07:18,714
Its size after one step is exactly square root of three.

111
00:07:18,714 --> 00:07:21,376
And in the gap, p is negative.

112
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:26,000
So p of b one is minus square root of three.

113
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:30,568
Now take any start between square root of three and b one.

114
00:07:30,568 --> 00:07:35,097
It is to the left of the crossing, so its step lands below the line,

115
00:07:35,097 --> 00:07:36,496
on the negative side.

116
00:07:36,496 --> 00:07:40,582
That is somewhere between minus square root of three and zero.

117
00:07:40,582 --> 00:07:45,098
It is inside, and it is negative, so it ends at minus one.

118
00:07:45,098 --> 00:07:46,420
One flip.

119
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:49,112
This is the first stripe.

120
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:51,552
Where is b one exactly?

121
00:07:51,552 --> 00:07:55,542
Its size after one step must be square root of three.

122
00:07:55,542 --> 00:08:01,298
With the size factor, that is b, times b squared minus three, over two,

123
00:08:01,298 --> 00:08:03,568
equals square root of three.

124
00:08:03,568 --> 00:08:07,739
Multiply by two, and we get b cubed, minus three b,

125
00:08:07,739 --> 00:08:13,874
equals two times square root of three, which is about three point four six.

126
00:08:13,974 --> 00:08:18,190
There is an exact formula for this b, built from cube roots,

127
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:20,228
but it does not tell us much.

128
00:08:20,228 --> 00:08:22,120
So just try values.

129
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:26,392
Two point one gives two point nine six, which is too small.

130
00:08:26,392 --> 00:08:30,698
Two point two gives four point zero five, which is too big.

131
00:08:30,698 --> 00:08:35,432
Closing in between them gives about two point one four eight.

132
00:08:35,883 --> 00:08:37,645
That fits what we saw.

133
00:08:37,645 --> 00:08:43,192
One point eight and two point zero are both below b one, in the first stripe,

134
00:08:43,192 --> 00:08:45,209
and both ended at minus one.

135
00:08:45,209 --> 00:08:47,901
Two point two is above b one.

136
00:08:47,901 --> 00:08:52,977
Its first step did not get inside, and it needed a second flip.

137
00:08:55,716 --> 00:08:57,982
Now the starts above b one.

138
00:08:57,982 --> 00:09:00,650
Their first step does not get inside.

139
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:03,478
But we do not have to follow them all the way.

140
00:09:03,478 --> 00:09:08,692
Suppose the first step lands, in size, somewhere in the first stripe.

141
00:09:08,692 --> 00:09:10,992
We already know the first stripe.

142
00:09:10,992 --> 00:09:14,000
From there it takes one more flip to get inside.

143
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,778
So such a start flips exactly twice.

144
00:09:18,216 --> 00:09:19,998
Which starts are those?

145
00:09:19,998 --> 00:09:21,800
Read it off the picture.

146
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,268
On the vertical axis,

147
00:09:23,268 --> 00:09:28,578
the first stripe is the band of sizes from square root of three up to b one.

148
00:09:28,578 --> 00:09:33,358
The folded curve only goes up, so it passes through that band once.

149
00:09:33,358 --> 00:09:37,708
It enters at b one, where its height is square root of three.

150
00:09:37,708 --> 00:09:41,082
And it leaves where its height is exactly b one.

151
00:09:41,082 --> 00:09:43,714
Call that point b two.

152
00:09:43,814 --> 00:09:47,222
So the size of p of b two is b one.

153
00:09:47,222 --> 00:09:53,756
The start is positive and the step flips it, so p of b two is minus b one.

154
00:09:53,856 --> 00:09:57,650
Now take any start between b one and b two.

155
00:09:57,650 --> 00:10:02,686
Its step lands between minus b one and minus square root of three.

156
00:10:02,686 --> 00:10:05,340
That is the first stripe, mirrored.

157
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:10,474
From there it takes one more flip, so two flips in all, an even number.

158
00:10:10,474 --> 00:10:12,246
It ends at plus one.

159
00:10:12,246 --> 00:10:14,978
This is the second stripe.

160
00:10:15,416 --> 00:10:17,572
Check it with two point two.

161
00:10:17,572 --> 00:10:20,602
It sits between b one and b two.

162
00:10:20,602 --> 00:10:24,232
Its first step has size two point zero two.

163
00:10:24,232 --> 00:10:29,760
And two point zero two lies between square root of three and b one,

164
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:31,410
in the first stripe.

165
00:10:31,510 --> 00:10:34,370
So one more flip, and it is inside.

166
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:39,040
That is the two flips we counted, and it ended at plus one.

167
00:10:39,483 --> 00:10:41,731
And the same step works again.

168
00:10:41,731 --> 00:10:47,138
Starts that land in the second stripe need one flip more than the second stripe

169
00:10:47,138 --> 00:10:48,575
does, so three flips.

170
00:10:48,575 --> 00:10:52,339
The curve leaves that band where its height is b two.

171
00:10:52,339 --> 00:10:54,281
Call that point b three.

172
00:10:54,281 --> 00:10:57,555
So p of b three is minus b two.

173
00:10:57,555 --> 00:11:02,278
The starts between b two and b three are the third stripe,

174
00:11:02,278 --> 00:11:04,395
and they end at minus one.

175
00:11:04,495 --> 00:11:11,190
Put the three edges next to each other. p of b one is minus square root of three.

176
00:11:11,190 --> 00:11:15,737
p of b two is minus b one. p of b three is minus b two.

177
00:11:15,737 --> 00:11:21,621
One step sends each edge to the edge before it, with the sign flipped.

178
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:24,163
And nothing stops us from going on.

179
00:11:24,163 --> 00:11:26,539
So we get a whole list of edges.

180
00:11:26,539 --> 00:11:32,475
Square root of three, then b one, b two, b three, and so on.

181
00:11:32,475 --> 00:11:36,574
Each new stripe is carried onto the stripe before it,

182
00:11:36,574 --> 00:11:39,203
so it needs exactly one more flip.

183
00:11:39,650 --> 00:11:43,129
Each edge is found like b one, by trying values.

184
00:11:43,129 --> 00:11:48,783
b one is about two point one four eight. b two is about two point two two one.

185
00:11:48,783 --> 00:11:51,828
b three is about two point two three four.

186
00:11:51,828 --> 00:11:58,136
They creep up toward square root of five, which is two point two three six.

187
00:11:59,383 --> 00:12:01,979
So here are all the stripes on one line.

188
00:12:01,979 --> 00:12:05,113
Stripe number n flips n times.

189
00:12:05,113 --> 00:12:10,529
An odd n ends at minus one, and an even n ends at plus one.

190
00:12:10,529 --> 00:12:13,651
That is the striped picture we saw.

191
00:12:14,083 --> 00:12:17,595
The stripes pile up against square root of five.

192
00:12:17,595 --> 00:12:22,585
Zoom in, and the same pattern shows up again and again.

193
00:12:23,016 --> 00:12:25,642
Why do the stripes get thin so quickly?

194
00:12:25,642 --> 00:12:29,530
Near square root of five the folded curve is steep.

195
00:12:29,530 --> 00:12:36,106
From two point two to two point two three, the start moves by zero point zero three.

196
00:12:36,206 --> 00:12:41,448
But the size after the step moves from two point zero two to two point two zero,

197
00:12:41,448 --> 00:12:43,414
which is zero point one eight.

198
00:12:43,414 --> 00:12:46,366
That is six times as far.

199
00:12:46,466 --> 00:12:51,198
So one step stretches a stripe to about six times its width.

200
00:12:51,298 --> 00:12:53,320
Where does the six come from?

201
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:55,978
Do what we did at zero and at one.

202
00:12:55,978 --> 00:12:59,514
Put square root of five plus a small e into p,

203
00:12:59,514 --> 00:13:03,050
and drop the terms with e squared and e cubed.

204
00:13:03,050 --> 00:13:07,332
Out comes minus square root of five, minus six e.

205
00:13:07,332 --> 00:13:14,134
So next to square root of five, one step multiplies a small distance by six,

206
00:13:14,134 --> 00:13:14,850
exactly.

207
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:18,888
And the stretched stripe has to fit onto the stripe before it.

208
00:13:18,888 --> 00:13:23,345
So each stripe is about a sixth as wide as the one before,

209
00:13:23,345 --> 00:13:28,648
and the closer to square root of five, the closer to exactly a sixth.

210
00:13:31,383 --> 00:13:33,555
One question is still open.

211
00:13:33,555 --> 00:13:36,207
Do the stripes really fill the whole gap?

212
00:13:36,207 --> 00:13:41,123
Or is there a last piece, right next to square root of five,

213
00:13:41,123 --> 00:13:43,417
that no stripe ever reaches?

214
00:13:43,517 --> 00:13:45,993
The stripes end at the edges.

215
00:13:45,993 --> 00:13:52,414
So this is a question about our list of edges, square root of three, b one, b two,

216
00:13:52,414 --> 00:13:53,197
and so on.

217
00:13:53,197 --> 00:13:57,493
Do the edges get all the way up to square root of five?

218
00:13:57,593 --> 00:13:59,365
Look at how we found them.

219
00:13:59,365 --> 00:14:03,413
Here is the corner of the picture next to square root of five.

220
00:14:03,413 --> 00:14:08,493
Start at the height square root of three, and go across to the folded curve.

221
00:14:08,493 --> 00:14:10,085
That is b one.

222
00:14:10,085 --> 00:14:13,881
Go up to the diagonal, and across to the curve again.

223
00:14:13,881 --> 00:14:15,503
That is b two.

224
00:14:15,503 --> 00:14:21,235
The edges are a staircase, squeezed between the curve and the diagonal.

225
00:14:22,883 --> 00:14:26,835
First, can an edge ever reach square root of five?

226
00:14:26,835 --> 00:14:28,727
The folded curve only goes up,

227
00:14:28,727 --> 00:14:33,647
and it gets to the height square root of five only at the very end of the gap.

228
00:14:33,647 --> 00:14:38,409
Each new edge is where the curve reaches the height of the edge before.

229
00:14:38,409 --> 00:14:41,308
If that height is below square root of five,

230
00:14:41,308 --> 00:14:44,405
the curve reaches it before the end of the gap.

231
00:14:44,405 --> 00:14:48,341
So the new edge is below square root of five as well.

232
00:14:48,341 --> 00:14:52,283
The list starts at square root of three, which is below.

233
00:14:52,283 --> 00:14:56,699
So every edge stays below square root of five.

234
00:14:56,799 --> 00:14:59,571
Next, does the list always go up?

235
00:14:59,571 --> 00:15:03,751
One step takes each edge to the size of the edge before it.

236
00:15:03,751 --> 00:15:08,915
Every edge is in the gap, and in the gap a step makes the size smaller.

237
00:15:08,915 --> 00:15:11,833
So the edge before is the smaller one.

238
00:15:11,833 --> 00:15:15,013
Each edge is bigger than the last.

239
00:15:15,113 --> 00:15:20,025
So the edges keep rising, and they can never pass square root of five.

240
00:15:20,025 --> 00:15:24,949
A list of numbers that only rises, under a ceiling it cannot pass,

241
00:15:24,949 --> 00:15:27,337
has to settle toward some value.

242
00:15:27,337 --> 00:15:29,909
Call that value L.

243
00:15:30,009 --> 00:15:31,371
Where is L?

244
00:15:31,371 --> 00:15:36,989
Far down the list, an edge and the one after it are both as close to L as we like.

245
00:15:36,989 --> 00:15:40,875
One step takes the later one to the size of the earlier one.

246
00:15:40,875 --> 00:15:42,531
Now p is a polynomial,

247
00:15:42,531 --> 00:15:47,727
so a tiny change in the input makes only a tiny change in the output.

248
00:15:47,727 --> 00:15:53,151
So one step from L itself lands as close as we like to a value of size L.

249
00:15:53,151 --> 00:15:57,471
And a fixed number that is as close as we like to L can only be L.

250
00:15:57,471 --> 00:16:01,671
So one step takes L to a value of the same size, L.

251
00:16:01,671 --> 00:16:05,323
That means the size factor at L is exactly one.

252
00:16:05,323 --> 00:16:07,235
We have met that before.

253
00:16:07,235 --> 00:16:13,199
L squared minus three, over two, equals one, so L squared is five.

254
00:16:13,199 --> 00:16:15,521
L is square root of five.

255
00:16:15,521 --> 00:16:20,663
On the picture, that is where the folded curve meets the diagonal.

256
00:16:20,763 --> 00:16:24,727
So the edges come as close to square root of five as we like.

257
00:16:24,727 --> 00:16:29,009
Every start below square root of five is passed by some edge,

258
00:16:29,009 --> 00:16:30,835
so it lies in some stripe.

259
00:16:30,835 --> 00:16:33,035
The stripes fill the whole gap.

260
00:16:33,035 --> 00:16:37,185
We now know where every start in the gap ends up.

261
00:16:39,616 --> 00:16:42,064
And what about the edges themselves?

262
00:16:42,064 --> 00:16:43,546
Take b one.

263
00:16:43,546 --> 00:16:48,150
Its first step lands exactly on minus square root of three.

264
00:16:48,150 --> 00:16:51,242
And we know what happens at square root of three.

265
00:16:51,242 --> 00:16:53,576
The next step gives exactly zero,

266
00:16:53,576 --> 00:16:58,456
and by the mirror rule the same holds for minus square root of three.

267
00:16:58,456 --> 00:17:02,412
So b one ends on zero, and stays there.

268
00:17:02,850 --> 00:17:04,864
b two lands on minus b one,

269
00:17:04,864 --> 00:17:09,790
and from there it follows the path of b one with the sign flipped.

270
00:17:09,790 --> 00:17:11,962
So it takes one step more.

271
00:17:11,962 --> 00:17:14,928
And b three takes one step more again.

272
00:17:14,928 --> 00:17:20,725
Every edge reaches plus or minus square root of three after a few steps,

273
00:17:20,725 --> 00:17:23,382
and then it sits on zero forever.

274
00:17:23,482 --> 00:17:27,520
An edge never reaches one or minus one.

275
00:17:27,983 --> 00:17:29,755
Should that worry us?

276
00:17:29,755 --> 00:17:31,197
Not much.

277
00:17:31,197 --> 00:17:35,527
Each edge is a single point, and zero is the top of the hill.

278
00:17:35,527 --> 00:17:38,294
Move the start by the tiniest amount,

279
00:17:38,294 --> 00:17:42,109
and it is inside a stripe on one side or the other,

280
00:17:42,109 --> 00:17:45,325
and it rolls down to plus one or minus one.

281
00:17:47,050 --> 00:17:49,734
Now we can answer the question we started with.

282
00:17:49,734 --> 00:17:54,640
Where does a positive sigma end up when we apply p again and again?

283
00:17:54,640 --> 00:18:01,096
Here is the full answer to part (e), from left to right along the number line.

284
00:18:01,550 --> 00:18:06,714
Below square root of three, the first step lands between zero and one,

285
00:18:06,714 --> 00:18:09,666
and from there sigma climbs to plus one.

286
00:18:09,666 --> 00:18:15,634
Exactly at square root of three, it lands on zero and stays there.

287
00:18:16,083 --> 00:18:19,807
Between square root of three and square root of five,

288
00:18:19,807 --> 00:18:23,039
each step flips the sign and shrinks the size,

289
00:18:23,039 --> 00:18:26,341
until the value is inside square root of three.

290
00:18:26,341 --> 00:18:31,741
An odd number of flips ends at minus one, and an even number ends at plus one.

291
00:18:31,741 --> 00:18:33,663
That gives the stripes.

292
00:18:33,663 --> 00:18:37,553
Their edges are single points that end on zero.

293
00:18:38,016 --> 00:18:40,291
Exactly at square root of five,

294
00:18:40,291 --> 00:18:44,914
sigma jumps between plus and minus square root of five forever.

295
00:18:44,914 --> 00:18:51,678
And beyond square root of five, each step flips and grows, so it explodes.

296
00:18:51,778 --> 00:18:56,834
Out of all these cases, only the first one is what we wanted.

297
00:18:58,883 --> 00:19:01,936
We wanted every singular value to end at one,

298
00:19:01,936 --> 00:19:06,007
and that only happens for starts below square root of three.

299
00:19:06,007 --> 00:19:09,781
But a real W can have singular values anywhere,

300
00:19:09,781 --> 00:19:13,235
some of them far above square root of five.

301
00:19:13,683 --> 00:19:17,393
This one, at two point nine, would explode.

302
00:19:17,393 --> 00:19:21,473
And one that sits in a gold stripe would end at minus one,

303
00:19:21,473 --> 00:19:23,443
with its direction reversed.

304
00:19:23,443 --> 00:19:28,853
So before we iterate, we have to bring every singular value down.

305
00:19:28,953 --> 00:19:30,755
That is easy to do.

306
00:19:30,755 --> 00:19:36,505
Divide W by a number, and every singular value is divided by that number,

307
00:19:36,505 --> 00:19:40,129
while U and V transpose stay exactly the same.

308
00:19:40,129 --> 00:19:45,405
Our target, U times V transpose, does not change at all.

309
00:19:45,850 --> 00:19:47,212
Which number?

310
00:19:47,212 --> 00:19:51,160
Dividing by the largest singular value would be ideal,

311
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:54,304
because then everything is at one or below.

312
00:19:54,304 --> 00:20:00,982
But finding the largest singular value takes the very SVD we are trying to avoid.

313
00:20:01,082 --> 00:20:04,710
There is a cheap stand-in, called the Frobenius norm.

314
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:10,004
Square every entry of W, add them all up, and take the square root.

315
00:20:10,004 --> 00:20:14,814
That only needs the entries, so it costs almost nothing.

316
00:20:14,914 --> 00:20:16,616
And it is big enough.

317
00:20:16,616 --> 00:20:18,028
Here is why.

318
00:20:18,028 --> 00:20:23,254
The sum of the squared entries of a matrix is the sum of the squared lengths of its

319
00:20:23,254 --> 00:20:23,758
columns.

320
00:20:23,758 --> 00:20:28,519
A rotation on the left turns every column without changing its length,

321
00:20:28,519 --> 00:20:30,356
so that sum stays the same.

322
00:20:30,356 --> 00:20:33,932
A rotation on the right does the same to the rows.

323
00:20:33,932 --> 00:20:42,016
So W, which is U, sigma, V transpose, has the same sum as sigma alone.

324
00:20:42,116 --> 00:20:44,913
And sigma holds only the singular values,

325
00:20:44,913 --> 00:20:50,372
so the sum of the squared entries equals the sum of the squared singular values.

326
00:20:50,372 --> 00:20:54,946
That sum already contains the largest one squared, plus more.

327
00:20:54,946 --> 00:20:58,212
So its square root, the Frobenius norm,

328
00:20:58,212 --> 00:21:02,316
is never smaller than the largest singular value.

329
00:21:02,750 --> 00:21:06,855
Here the Frobenius norm comes out to about three point four five,

330
00:21:06,855 --> 00:21:10,582
a bit more than our largest singular value, two point nine.

331
00:21:10,582 --> 00:21:13,690
Divide every sigma by it.

332
00:21:14,150 --> 00:21:19,478
Two point nine becomes zero point eight four, and the others are smaller still.

333
00:21:19,478 --> 00:21:24,000
Now every singular value sits between zero and one,

334
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,016
safely below square root of three.

335
00:21:27,450 --> 00:21:35,129
Now apply our matrix step, three halves W, minus one half W, W transpose, W,

336
00:21:35,129 --> 00:21:36,746
again and again.

337
00:21:36,746 --> 00:21:39,664
It needs nothing but matrix products.

338
00:21:39,664 --> 00:21:45,002
And each time, every singular value goes through p once.

339
00:21:45,102 --> 00:21:48,572
So each singular value climbs to plus one.

340
00:21:48,572 --> 00:21:50,445
The small ones start slowly,

341
00:21:50,445 --> 00:21:55,063
growing by about one and a half times per step as zero point one did,

342
00:21:55,063 --> 00:21:56,402
but they all arrive.

343
00:21:56,402 --> 00:22:00,660
The one exception is a singular value of exactly zero.

344
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:05,150
Zero is a fixed point, so it stays at zero.

345
00:22:05,250 --> 00:22:09,270
And all along, U and V transpose never changed.

346
00:22:09,270 --> 00:22:15,011
So when no singular value is zero, the middle is now all ones,

347
00:22:15,011 --> 00:22:18,438
and W has become U times V transpose.

348
00:22:18,883 --> 00:22:21,321
The ellipse has become a circle.

349
00:22:21,321 --> 00:22:27,225
All it took was matrix products, and a cubic built from two simple wishes.

350
00:22:29,250 --> 00:22:31,778
So the whole method is two lines.

351
00:22:31,778 --> 00:22:35,828
Divide W by its Frobenius norm, once.

352
00:22:35,828 --> 00:22:39,842
Then apply the step, again and again.

353
00:22:40,283 --> 00:22:42,809
And where does a singular value go?

354
00:22:42,809 --> 00:22:47,447
After that first division, every one of them goes to one.

